Q: cs50 - pset2 - Can&#39;t pass the check A: <p>Reviewing the faltering check output, I compiled and ran your program using the noted key.  The first issue noted by the checker is most likely caused by the fact that only the first twenty-six characters of the inputted string are being evaluated, leaving the remainder of the input text (character array &quot;f&quot;) unprocessed and possibly containing spurious data.</p>
<pre><code>    for (i = 0; i &lt; strlen(argv[1]); i++)   /* Does not accommodate entered strings that are longer than 26 characters  */
    {
        if ((s[i] &gt; '@' &amp;&amp; s[i] &lt; '[') || (s[i] &gt; '`' &amp;&amp; s[i] &lt; '{'))
        {
            if (isupper(s[i]))
            {
                z = s[i] - 65;
                f[i] = toupper(argv[1][z]);
            }
            else if (islower(s[i]))
            {
                z = s[i] - 97;
                f[i] = tolower(argv[1][z]);
            }
        }
        else
        {
            f[i] = s[i];
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Following is an entered short text string followed by a text string that exceeds the length of the key.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encode/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encode DWUSXNPQKEGCZFJBTLYROHIAVM
plaintext: Hello there
ciphertext: Qxccj rqxlx

craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encode/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encode DWUSXNPQKEGCZFJBTLYROHIAVM
plaintext: Hello there.  This text string is longer than 26 characters.
ciphertext: Qxccj rqxlx.  Rqky rxar yr@j�
</code></pre>
<p>The other complaint by the checker appears to point back to the key validation loop in the program.</p>
<pre><code>    while (n &lt; strlen(argv[1]))
    {
        if ((argv[1][i] &gt; '@' &amp;&amp; argv[1][i] &lt; '[') || (argv[1][i] &gt; '`' &amp;&amp; argv[1][i] &lt; '{')) /* Allows numeric characters  */
        {
            n++;
        }
        else
        {
            printf(&quot;Key must only contain alphabetic characters.\n&quot;);
            return 1;
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>The &quot;if&quot; test is allowing for numeric characters ('0' through '9'), which probably is not to be allowed.</p>
<p>Following is a test of that scenario.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encode/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encode DWUSXNPQKEGCZFJB0123456789
plaintext: Wizards
ciphertext: 6k9d1s2
</code></pre>
<p>With those complaints identified, following is a refactored version of the code to address those complaints.</p>
<pre><code>int main(int argc, string argv[])
{
    int i = 0, z, n = 0;
    if (argc != 2)
    {
        printf(&quot;Usage: ./substitution key\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    else if (strlen(argv[1]) != 26)
    {
        printf(&quot;Key must contain 26 characters.\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    while (n &lt; strlen(argv[1]))
    {
        if (isalpha(argv[1][n]))    /* Only allow alphabetic characters     */
        {
            n++;
        }
        else
        {
            printf(&quot;Key must only contain alphabetic characters.\n&quot;);
            return 1;
        }
    }
    for (i = 0; i &lt; strlen(argv[1]); i++)
    {
        for (n = 0; n &lt; strlen(argv[1]); n++)
        {
            if (tolower(argv[1][i]) == tolower(argv[1][n]) &amp;&amp; i != n)
            {
                printf(&quot;Key must not contain repeated characters.\n&quot;);
                return 1;
            }
        }
    }
    string s = get_string(&quot;plaintext.: &quot;);
    char f[strlen(s) + 1];                  /* Accomodate a null terminator at the end of the string    */
    f[strlen(s)] = '\0';
    for (i = 0; i &lt; strlen(s); i++)         /* Correct to read all entered text characters              */
    {
        if (isalpha(s[i]))
        {
            if (isupper(s[i]))
            {
                z = s[i] - 'A';             /* Clarifies the ASCII character adjustment to an offset    */
                f[i] = toupper(argv[1][z]);
            }
            else if (islower(s[i]))
            {
                z = s[i] - 'a';             /* Clarifies the ASCII character adjustment to an offset    */
                f[i] = tolower(argv[1][z]);
            }
        }
        else
        {
            f[i] = s[i];
        }
    }
    printf(&quot;ciphertext: &quot;);
    for (i = 0; i &lt; strlen(f); i++)
    {
        printf(&quot;%c&quot; , f[i]);
    }
    printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>And, following are some tests to mimic the tests that elicited the checker complaints.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encode/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encode DWUSXNPQKEGCZFJBTLYROHIAVM
plaintext.: Hee is another text string that is longer than twenty-six characters.
ciphertext: Qxx ky dfjrqxl rxar yrlkfp rqdr ky cjfpxl rqdf rixfrv-yka uqdldurxly.
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encode/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encode DWUSXNPQKEGCZFJBT012345678
Key must only contain alphabetic characters.
</code></pre>
<p>The key takeaway here is probably more study of &quot;C&quot; tutorials should be done to understand characters and character arrays, along with the functionality available within the &lt;ctype.h&gt; function package.</p>

Q: Write fix point function in Rust A: <p>Rust's Fn, FnMut, and FnOnce are intrinsic traits that allow direct use of the func(...args) function application syntax. Here, I'm using the apply method as a substitute,
attempting to slightly improve the syntax with Rust declarative macros, although the function body still leaves something to be desired.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>struct Fix&lt;'fix, A, B&gt; {
    thunk: &amp;'fix dyn Fn(&amp;Fix&lt;A, B&gt;, A) -&gt; B,
}

impl&lt;'fix, A, B&gt; Fix&lt;'fix, A, B&gt; {
    fn new(thunk: &amp;'fix dyn Fn(&amp;Fix&lt;A, B&gt;, A) -&gt; B) -&gt; Fix&lt;'fix, A, B&gt; {
        Fix { thunk }
    }
    fn apply(&amp;self, x: A) -&gt; B {
        (self.thunk)(self, x)
    }
}

macro_rules! fix {
    (

          $this : ident : fn($input : ty) -&gt; $output : ty,
          $arg : ident : $input_ : ty
       =&gt;   $body : expr
    ) =&gt; {
        {
          let func = Fix::new(
            &amp;| $this : &amp;Fix&lt;$input, $output&gt;,
                $arg: $input_ | -&gt; $output {
             $body
          });
          move |$arg : $input_| { func.apply($arg) }
        }

    };
}

fn main() {
    // raw example
    let fib = Fix::new(&amp;|this: &amp;Fix&lt;i32, i32&gt;, n: i32| -&gt; i32 {
        if n &lt; 2 {
            1
        } else {
            this.apply(n - 1) + this.apply(n - 2)
        }
    });
    let fib = move |n: i32| fib.apply(n);
    println!(&quot;fib: {:?}&quot;, fib(10));

    // raw example
    let fib = fix!(this : fn(i32) -&gt; i32, n : i32 =&gt; {
        if n &lt; 2 { 1 } else { this.apply(n-1) + this.apply(n-2) }
      }
    );
    println!(&quot;fib: {:?}&quot;, fib(10));

}
</code></pre>
